/* book Worked example 2: Q: A wing skin panel between two stringers and two ribs is 450 mm long (along the load) and 300 mm wide, 2 mm thick, with E = 72 GPa and ν = 0.33. Take all edges simply supported. Find k, the mode and σcr. Compare with the long-plate value, and with a stringer flange of the same width and thickness that has one free edge.
   1. r = 450/300 = 1.5, between the crossing points 1.41 and 2.45, so m = 2 should control.
   2. k: m = 1 gives (0.667 + 1.5)² = 4.694; m = 2 gives (1.333 + 0.75)² = 4.340; m = 3 gives (2 + 0.5)² = 6.250. So k = 4.34 with m = 2.
   3. σcr = 4.34 × π² × 72,000/(12 × 0.8911) × (2/300)² = 4.34 × 66,455 × 4.444e-05 = 12.8 N/mm².
   4. Long-plate value with k = 4: 11.8 N/mm², 8% lower; the short panel gains a little from its ribs.
   5. Flange with one free edge, k = 0.425: σcr = 1.26 N/mm², about a tenth of the skin value. A flange of this width would be useless; flanges are made much narrower.
   Answer: k = 4.34 with two half-waves; σcr = 12.8 N/mm² (long-plate 11.8, free-edge flange 1.26 N/mm²). */
