/* book: lead: A stiffened panel is a skin with stringers running along the load. In compression the thin skin buckles between the stringers long before the stringers fail. The buckled skin still works, but mostly in narrow strips next to each stringer. The effective width method replaces the real, uneven stress in the skin by a strip of width be working at the stringer stress. Wing upper surfaces, fuselage crow */
/* book Figure 6.4.1: The worked panel at a stringer stress of 100 MPa. The real skin stress (shaded curve) stays at 100 MPa at each stringer and falls towards the bay buckling stress of 25.3 MPa at mid-bay. Von Kármán's effective width replaces it by strips totalling be = 75.4 mm per stringer, 37.7 mm each side, at the  */
