/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A flat sheet alone is a poor compression member: its buckling stress varies as (t/b)², so a wide thin sheet buckles at a few MPa.
   - Above σcr the middle of each bay bows out of plane and stops taking extra load.
   - Von Kármán replaced the uneven stress by two strips of sheet, one each side of the stringer, working at the full stringer stress σs, with the rest of the bay carrying nothing.
   - be is the total effective width of sheet working with one stringer, half on each side (mm); t the sheet thickness (mm); E Young's modulus and σs the stringer stress, in the same unit; the 1.90 is for ν = 0.3.
   - As σs rises, be shrinks: the more the panel is loaded, the more the sheet sheds load to the stringers.
   - Ae is the effective area per stringer (mm²); Ast the stringer area (mm²); P the load carried by one stringer and its sheet (N).
   - The 1.90 assumes the stringers hold the bay edges straight and give simple support.
   - Shear lag is a second reason why a wide skin is not fully effective, and it happens even without buckling.
*/
