/* book Worked example: Q: A compression panel has stringers of area 200 mm² at a pitch of 150 mm on a 1.5 mm skin, with E = 70 GPa and ν = 0.3. Find the stress at which the skin bays buckle. When the stringer stress is 100 MPa, find the effective width, the effective area and the load per stringer, and compare with a skin that did not buckle.
   1. Sheet buckling, k = 4: σcr = 4π²E/[12(1 − ν²)] × (t/p)² = 3.615 × 70,000 × (1.5/150)² = 25.3 MPa.
   2. At σs = 100 MPa the sheet has buckled, since 100 > 25.3.
   3. E/σs = 70,000/100 = 700, √700 = 26.46. be = 1.90 × 1.5 × 26.46 = 75.4 mm, 37.7 mm each side of the stringer; it is less than p = 150 mm, so the formula applies.
   4. Effective area: Ae = 200 + 75.4 × 1.5 = 200 + 113.1 = 313.1 mm².
   5. Load per stringer: P = 100 × 313.1 = 31,311 N = 31.3 kN; the sheet carries 11.3 kN of it.
   6. Average sheet stress over the bay: 100 × 75.4/150 = 50.3 MPa, against 100 MPa at the stringer.
   7. If the sheet had not buckled, the whole 150 mm would work: A = 200 + 225 = 425 mm² and P = 42.5 kN. Buckling costs 26% of the load at this stress.
   Answer: σcr = 25.3 MPa; at 100 MPa, be = 75.4 mm (37.7 mm each side), Ae = 313.1 mm² and P = 31.3 kN per stringer. */
