/* book exam traps: Taking 1.90 t √(E/σ) as the width on each side. It is the total width working with one stringer; each side gets half. | Using the effective width formula before the sheet has buckled, or keeping be larger than the pitch. Below σcr, or when be > p, the whole bay is effective. | Using the yield stress, or the sheet buckling stress, for σ in the formula. Use the stringer stress at the load case being checked. | Mixing units inside √(E/σ). E and σ must be in the same unit; be comes out in the unit of t. | Thinking shear lag needs buckling. Shear lag reduces skin effectiveness in an unbuckled box near roots and cut-outs, through shear strain in the skin. */
